## Project context

CreteValleyDSS uses plugin-owned Evaluation Structures.

Evaluation payloads are intentionally persisted using Mongoose `Schema.Types.Mixed`, so there is no universal evaluation payload shape enforced by the database.

This flexibility does NOT mean that arbitrary payloads are valid.

This Evaluation Structure owns:

- its canonical payload shape;
- structure-specific validation;
- normalization before persistence;
- draft versus submit requirements.

Do not modify shared persistence schemas merely to support this plugin.

Do not add structure-specific fields to shared `Issue` or `IssueEvaluation` models.

## Backend errors and shared utilities

Use the repository's existing Backend error contract.

Do not throw plain `Error` or `TypeError` for expected validation failures or known application invariants when an existing `AppError` factory is appropriate.

Use the factories from:

```text
Backend/utils/common/errors.js
```

In particular:

- `createBadRequestError(...)` for invalid user-controlled or persisted evaluation input;
- `createInternalError(...)` for malformed/inconsistent application-provided runtime context.

Examples of `BAD_REQUEST` conditions include malformed payload shape, invalid field values, duplicate submitted values, unknown referenced IDs, incomplete submit data and unsupported evaluation save modes.

Examples of `INTERNAL_ERROR` conditions include malformed `decisionContext`, invalid canonical collections and duplicate/invalid canonical IDs supplied by the application itself.

Include `field` metadata whenever the failing location is known.

Use `details` when structured diagnostic data is useful.

Reuse common helpers from:

```text
Backend/utils/common/objects.js
Backend/utils/common/strings.js
```

instead of locally recreating equivalent generic helpers.

When only the standard draft/submit "required value" distinction is needed, prefer:

```text
Backend/modules/decisionPlugins/evaluations/shared/resolveRequireValue.js
```

and its `resolveRequireValue(mode)` API.

Do not force that helper when the structure genuinely needs more specific draft/submit semantics.

## Backend implementation style

Match existing CreteValleyDSS Backend conventions:

- ES modules and repository import conventions;
- existing shared utilities before local duplicates;
- structure-specific logic kept inside the structure package;
- pure operations in `operations/` only when they make the implementation clearer;
- no mutation of caller-owned inputs;
- deliberate normalization instead of broad coercion;
- meaningful error fields/details;
- no compatibility fallbacks or legacy aliases unless required by an existing public contract;
- no generic abstractions created for a single use;
- no new dependency when existing repository/platform APIs are sufficient.

Prefer the simplest implementation that looks native to the surrounding Backend.

## Public Backend contract

Preserve the generated public integration contract.

### GET

```js
async ({
  payload,
  decisionContext,
}) => frontendPayload
```

The GET handler prepares the complete payload consumed by the Frontend.

If the canonical stored payload already has the correct shape, do not transform it unnecessarily.

### SAVE

```js
async ({
  payload,
  decisionContext,
  mode,
}) => storedPayload
```

`mode` is `"draft"` or `"submit"`.

Draft may allow incomplete data when appropriate.

Submit must enforce the definitive submission rules.

Return the complete normalized payload that should be persisted.

Do not mutate the input payload.

